[engine] harness activity stderr task=DOC-1.4 attempt=2 at=2026-10-30T20:48:10.630Z === artifact history ===
[engine] harness activity stderr task=DOC-1.4 attempt=2 at=2026-10-30T20:48:10.630Z The v0.1.0 release gate exercises the four outcomes below on a fresh Linux host with rootless Podman `>= 5.7.0, < 6.0.0`, lingering enabled, and an empty state root. The [release-gate record](docs/reviews/v1-success-scenarios-evidence.md) contains the commands, exit codes, and observed output.
[engine] harness activity stderr task=DOC-1.4 attempt=2 at=2026-10-30T20:48:10.630Z ### 1. The app is reachable from another machine
[engine] harness activity stderr task=DOC-1.4 attempt=2 at=2026-10-30T20:48:10.630Z ```sh
[engine] harness activity stderr task=DOC-1.4 attempt=2 at=2026-10-30T20:48:10.630Z easy-host add https://github.com/your-org/your-app.git --name blog --port 8080 --yes
[engine] harness activity stderr task=DOC-1.4 attempt=2 at=2026-10-30T20:48:10.630Z curl -fsS http://<host-lan-address>:8080/
[engine] harness activity stderr task=DOC-1.4 attempt=2 at=2026-10-30T20:48:10.630Z ```
[engine] harness activity stderr task=DOC-1.4 attempt=2 at=2026-10-30T20:48:10.630Z The `add` exits `0` and the app answers on the published port from a second machine on the LAN. A request to the host's own loopback from that second machine is not expected to answer; use the host's LAN address.
[engine] harness activity stderr task=DOC-1.4 attempt=2 at=2026-10-30T20:48:10.630Z ### 2. The app returns after a reboot
[engine] harness activity stderr task=DOC-1.4 attempt=2 at=2026-10-30T20:48:10.630Z Reboot the host and wait for it to come back. With lingering enabled, easy-host has enabled `podman-restart.service`, which replays `--restart=always` containers at boot. No easy-host command is required, and the app answers on the same port.
[engine] harness activity stderr task=DOC-1.4 attempt=2 at=2026-10-30T20:48:10.630Z ### 3. A broken update keeps the previous version serving
[engine] harness activity stderr task=DOC-1.4 attempt=2 at=2026-10-30T20:48:10.630Z ```sh
[engine] harness activity stderr task=DOC-1.4 attempt=2 at=2026-10-30T20:48:10.630Z git push origin main
[engine] harness activity stderr task=DOC-1.4 attempt=2 at=2026-10-30T20:48:10.630Z easy-host update blog
[engine] harness activity stderr task=DOC-1.4 attempt=2 at=2026-10-30T20:48:10.630Z ```
[engine] harness activity stderr task=DOC-1.4 attempt=2 at=2026-10-30T20:48:10.630Z If the candidate image fails to build, `update` exits `3` and the old container is untouched. If the candidate starts but fails the readiness probe, `update` exits `4`, stops the candidate, restarts the previous image, and reports `state: reverted`. In both cases the previous version keeps serving and `status` records the failure.
[engine] harness activity stderr task=DOC-1.4 attempt=2 at=2026-10-30T20:48:10.630Z ### 4. Rollback restores the previous image
[engine] harness activity stderr task=DOC-1.4 attempt=2 at=2026-10-30T20:48:10.630Z ```sh
[engine] harness activity stderr task=DOC-1.4 attempt=2 at=2026-10-30T20:48:10.630Z easy-host rollback blog
[engine] harness activity stderr task=DOC-1.4 attempt=2 at=2026-10-30T20:48:10.630Z easy-host rollback blog
[engine] harness activity stderr task=DOC-1.4 attempt=2 at=2026-10-30T20:48:10.630Z ```
[engine] harness activity stderr task=DOC-1.4 attempt=2 at=2026-10-30T20:48:10.630Z The first rollback swaps the current and previous images, probes the restored image, and exits `0` when it serves. The second rollback returns to the newer image. easy-host retains exactly two images per app; a failed rollback stops the candidate and restarts the image it was trying to leave.
[engine] harness activity stderr task=DOC-1.4 attempt=2 at=2026-10-30T20:48:10.630Z ## Volume safety
[engine] harness activity stderr task=DOC-1.4 attempt=2 at=2026-10-30T20:48:10.630Z Declare persistent data as a named volume when you add the app, for example `--volume blog-data:/data`. The same declaration can live in the app's `[volumes]` table. Named volumes survive `update` and `rollback`.
[engine] harness activity stderr task=DOC-1.4 attempt=2 at=2026-10-30T20:48:10.630Z `easy-host remove blog` deletes the container, workspace, app directory, and images, but keeps every named volume and prints the exact command to delete each one, for example:
[engine] harness activity stderr task=DOC-1.4 attempt=2 at=2026-10-30T20:48:10.630Z ```sh
[engine] harness activity stderr task=DOC-1.4 attempt=2 at=2026-10-30T20:48:10.630Z podman volume rm easy-host-blog-blog-data
[engine] harness activity stderr task=DOC-1.4 attempt=2 at=2026-10-30T20:48:10.631Z ```
[engine] harness activity stderr task=DOC-1.4 attempt=2 at=2026-10-30T20:48:10.631Z `easy-host remove blog --volumes` deletes only the named volumes labelled for that app. It never deletes a host filesystem path, and it refuses a volume whose name merely matches the app pattern but whose `io.easy-host.app` label is missing or different. `--force` skips confirmation prompts but does not imply `--volumes`; without `--force`, a non-interactive `remove` requires `--yes`.
